# 1. Is the renewal-chain analysis correct?

Step by step, confirming most of it and refuting the phrasing of two steps.

**"Agent-not-thread changes visibility, not meaning" — confirmed, with one addition.** A renewer that is an agent is composed explicitly and its renewals are appended facts under its own authorship, so it becomes *judgeable*: a grantor's judge can observe the manager's behavior the way it observes anything else on the medium. That is a real gain — Jini's `LeaseRenewalManager` was invisible precisely because it was a thread inside the holder's process. But visibility does not change what a renewal asserts. My draft's prohibition ("never renewed by machinery") was aimed at a *behavior*, and an agent exhibiting that behavior is the same defect in cleaner materials. The prohibition as I wrote it names the materials, not the behavior; that is the one clause of the existing draft this question genuinely corrects (see §5).

**"Unconditional vs. on-evidence is the decisive question" — confirmed, and it is exactly the lease's own criterion applied to the renewer.** A renewal is evidence, and evidence has an author who must have observed something. An unconditional renewer observed nothing; its renewals are Jini's false liveness regardless of how honestly they are appended. An on-evidence renewer is doing what this document already calls judging.

**"The manager's liveness signal from the holder is a lease" — confirmed in the canonical form, refined per Axiom 2.** What the manager needs is evidence of the holder within a declared window — a judgment, of which a formal lease is the portable, disciplined form, not the only form. My previous draft already established that a judge may treat any attributed traffic from a party as evidence of life, with the explicit renewal fact as the transport-independent case. The links of the chain are *judgment windows*; leases are their canonical vocabulary. An implementer should not conclude that every link must mint `:dao.lease/…` facts; it must have a declared window and observe within it.

**"The holder leases from the manager and the manager leases from the grantor" — refuted as phrased.** The second half misassigns tenure. The manager does not become the holder of the upstream lease; the working party remains the holder — reclaim deprives the worker, release comes from the worker, `:dao.lease/max` caps the worker's tenure. The manager is only the *author of renewal evidence*. **Renewal chains delegate evidence, never tenure.** The structure where the manager literally holds the lease and sub-grants to the worker is sub-leasing/transfer — a different design, and one my draft's Out-of-scope list already defers ("transfer or delegation of a lease between holders waits for a real need"). Conflating the two is how a lease-manager quietly becomes a broker with tenure it never earned.

**"The chain's weakest link defines the guarantee" — a real rule, but as sloganed it is wrong in a checkable way.** "Weakest link" suggests the guarantee is the *minimum* over links. It is not; it is a **sum**. State it so an implementer can check it:

> A renewal is **grounded** iff its author is the party whose work the lease covers, or its author had observed, within its own declared tolerance window, a grounded renewal (or evidence its judge accepts as equivalent) of that party. What a grounded renewal proves to the grantor is: *the working party was alive at some moment no older than the sum of the tolerance windows along the chain.* An unconditional renewer has no window — the sum is unbounded — so everything downstream of it proves nothing.

That yields two mechanical checks. **Local:** every renewal append by a non-holder is guarded by a within-window observation predicate — structurally the same guard/deopt shape as everything else here: when the manager's window over the holder expires, the manager *stops renewing*, which is its deopt; silence propagates down the chain one window at a time. **Global:** the grantor's effective staleness bound for the actual work is its own duration plus the summed upstream windows — and the grantor cannot see chain depth, which is an honest cost: delegation widens the grantor's real tolerance without its knowledge. The slogan survives only as the degenerate observation that one unguarded link makes the sum infinite.

**"Batching and cadence decoupling are what it buys" — confirmed, but cadence decoupling must be stated honestly.** Batching is real and cheap: renewal is the trace half, and one appended fact carrying a set of lease ids is a legitimate trace shape (Jini's `LeaseMap` reduced to a value). Cadence decoupling does **not** tighten the end-to-end bound — by the sum rule it cannot; if the holder goes silent for its longest synchronous stretch, *someone's* window has to cover that stretch whether or not a manager exists. What it actually does is **relocate the wide window to the party with the best evidence**: the manager can sit close to the holder (same host, same medium, negligible transit delay, richer attributed traffic), carry the wide tolerance there, and let the remote grantor keep a tight duration against the manager's steady cadence. The grantor then detects *manager* death quickly and delegates detecting *holder* death to the party best placed to judge it. That is a real, but second-order, benefit.

**"A lease manager is an interpreter, not a component" — confirmed without reservation.** It reads lease facts through its own cursor and appends renewal facts, exactly the shape of `forward!` and the JIT. `dao.lease.md` gains no component, no operation, no key in any result map.

# 2. Is delegated renewal worth having at all?

**Worth stating, not worth building — in this slice, the discipline goes in the document and no mechanism goes anywhere.**

The case against building now: the benefits are scale benefits. Batching matters when one party renews many leases; evidence relocation matters when grantor and holder are far apart and a nearer judge exists. The system currently has zero deployed leases. Meanwhile the cost of *underspecifying* it is real and immediate: the question "what does a renewal from someone else mean" has no answer in the current draft, and a document that is silent there invites the unconditional renewer — it will be built by the first person who finds renewing inconvenient, as Jini proved.

The case against forbidding entirely: the grounding rule shows the on-evidence form is not a weakening of the lease but a composition of judges, each individually obeying the document's existing discipline. Forbidding it would forbid a correct shape to prevent a defective one, when the two are separable by a checkable predicate.

So the answer to "a chain in which liveness can be faked at every link": under the grounding rule it *cannot* be faked at any guarded link, only delayed by that link's window, and the sum of delays is declared and finite. The faking risk is entirely the unguarded link, and that is exactly what the document should prohibit by name — behavior, not materials. State the semantics now (a paragraph); mint no vocabulary; build nothing.

# 3. Does delegated renewal require Shibi?

**No.** Walk the candidates weakest-first:

**(a) No authority — the grantor counts any renewal naming the lease.** What it costs: anyone who can deposit on the medium can manufacture liveness for a dead holder indefinitely — denial-of-reclaim; a squatter keeps the resource held with the holder gone. `:dao.lease/max` bounds the damage but at the price of the cap doing admission's job. Note, though, what "anyone" means here: media are compositions, and the canonical deposit medium (`dao.stream.ws.md`) attributes every event to an attachment. There is no ambient shared pool; "no authority" in this architecture already means "anyone the composition let attach," which for a single-attachment private medium is no one else. So (a) is actually *sufficient for compositions where the medium's admission already restricts depositors to trusted parties* — and insufficient the moment the medium is shared.

**(b) A holder-authorized renewer in a grantor-visible fact.** The holder, while alive, appends a fact naming the delegate whose renewals the grantor's judge should count for this lease. **This is the weakest mechanism that is sufficient in general, and the reason is that the substrate already provides the hard part.** The authority question is one bit — "do R's appends count as evidence for lease L" — and the architecture already answers the undelegated version of that bit by *authorship*: the grantor counts the holder's renewals because streams are single-writer and attachments are attributed; authorship is the ambient capability this system has had all along. (b) extends that trust exactly one explicit, revocable, grantor-visible step: SDSI/SPKI-shaped local naming, no token, no attenuation machinery, verification by reading the medium. Revocation is a retraction fact or the lease ending. Scope is one lease, so attenuation has nothing to attenuate. What (b) cannot do is depth: the delegate's delegate needs a chained authorization, and chained, attenuable, offline-verifiable authorization *is* the Macaroon design in `dao.space.security.md` — that is the precise line where (b) ends and Shibi begins. Depth-1 delegation by fact; deeper waits.

**(c) A full capability token.** Needed only for what (b) lacks: multi-step delegation chains, attenuation, offline verification without reading the holder's stream, delegation to parties the grantor cannot otherwise attribute. None of these is in the slice.

One consequential nuance: whose renewals count is, finally, the grantor's judge's *interpretation* — Axiom 2. Mechanisms (a)–(c) are successively stronger conventions the judge may adopt, not gates the vocabulary enforces. The document should say that plainly: authority over renewal evidence is a judging policy, defaulting to authorship, extensible by a holder's delegation fact, upgradeable to Shibi — and the vocabulary is identical under all three.

# 4. Does this force the capability-or-currency decision?

**No, because leases decline to be Shibi's first consumer — and that declining is itself the discovery document's precondition honored, not dodged.** "Before ShiBi is load-bearing in any design, force the decision" has a contrapositive: a design that keeps Shibi non-load-bearing forces nothing and owes nothing. Since (b) suffices, `dao.lease.md` takes no dependency, and the decision stays unforced.

But leases do **add evidence**, and it is worth recording where: everything lease-shaped that touches authorization has capability physics and none has currency physics. A delegation is scoped to one function (renew), one object (this lease), time-bounded, revocable, and — decisively — **not spent**: appending a renewal consumes nothing, transfers nothing, and needs no double-spend resolution or global state. The enforcement split also matches `dao.space.security.md` exactly: were the delegation a token, the token would be authenticated cryptographically while the *predicate* — renewals count only within the window, only for this lease — is enforced by the substrate, namely the grantor's judge, which is this architecture's evaluator. A lease caveat is a caveat, not a coin. So: leases settle nothing, must stay out of it, and file one exhibit for "capability." If Shibi lands as a capability, the delegation fact of (b) is its degenerate depth-1, unattenuated form, and a token simply replaces the identity in that fact; if Shibi somehow landed as a currency, leases would be untouched, because they never depended on it. That upgrade-in-place is the test that the document below does not need rewriting.

# 5. What `dao.lease.md` should say now

Three edits to the draft in `architect-dao-lease-jit-pattern.claude-fable-5.stdout.log`. Exact text:

**(i) Replace the final clause of "What a lease may never do"** (from "And it is never renewed by machinery…" to the end of that paragraph) with:

> And it is never renewed **unconditionally**: renewal is evidence, and evidence has an author who observed something. A holder renews from its own control flow because its own execution is the thing observed. Machinery that renews on a schedule regardless of what it has observed — Jini's `LeaseRenewalManager` — manufactures exactly the false liveness the lease exists to end, and it does so whether it is a thread or an agent: composing the renewer explicitly changes its visibility, not what its renewals assert. Renewal by another party that *has* observed the holder is not this defect; see Delegated renewal.

**(ii) Insert a new section "Delegated renewal"** between "Renewal forever" and "Out of scope":

> ## Delegated renewal
>
> A renewal may be appended by a party other than the holder. A holder's renewal is evidence about itself; a delegate's renewal is a judgment about someone else, and it is evidence exactly as far as the delegate's own observation reaches:
>
> A renewal is **grounded** iff its author is the party whose work the lease covers, or its author observed, within its own declared tolerance window, a grounded renewal — or evidence its judge accepts as equivalent — of that party. What a grounded renewal proves to the grantor is that the working party was alive within the **sum of the windows along the chain**. A link that renews without observing has no window, the sum is unbounded, and everything downstream of it proves nothing; such a renewer is the prohibited machinery above, whatever it is made of. A guarded delegate needs no prohibition: when its window over the holder expires it stops renewing, and silence propagates down the chain one window at a time.
>
> Chains delegate **evidence, never tenure**. The working party remains the holder: reclaim deprives it, release is its to append, the cap bounds its tenure. A party that holds a lease and grants derived leases to others is sub-leasing, which is out of scope below.
>
> Whose renewals a grantor counts is its judge's interpretation, per Axiom 2, and the default is the authority the substrate already provides: **authorship**. The grantor counts the holder's renewals because they are attributably the holder's. A composition on a shared medium that wants delegation extends that by one explicit step — a holder-authored, grantor-visible fact naming the delegate — and that is sufficient: scope is one lease, revocation is a retraction or the lease's end, verification is reading the medium. This document mints no key for that fact; no composition needs it yet. It is recorded here so that the mechanism, when needed, is known to be a fact and an authorship check — not a capability token. Shibi is not required, and this document takes no dependency on Shibi and no position on its nature; if Shibi exists in capability form one day, a token naming the delegate replaces the identity in that fact, and nothing else here changes. Delegation deeper than one step — a delegate's delegate — requires chained, attenuable authorization, which is precisely where Shibi begins; it is deferred with it.
>
> What delegation buys, when a composition reaches for it: **batching** — renewal is the trace half, and one appended fact may carry a set of lease ids — and **evidence relocation**: the sum rule means a manager never tightens the end-to-end bound, but it moves the wide window to a judge sitting near the holder, with richer attributed traffic and negligible transit delay, letting the remote grantor hold a tight duration against the manager's cadence. A lease manager is therefore not a component: it is an interpreter that reads lease facts and appends renewal facts, the same shape as every other judge in this document, and it appears in no contract.

**(iii) Two hygiene edits that remove the draft's only Shibi load-bearing risk.** In "Renewal forever," change "distinguishing them is admission policy — who gets `:accepted` at all, eventually gated by Shibi — never lease vocabulary" to "distinguishing them is admission policy — who gets `:accepted` at all, which belongs to the composition — never lease vocabulary." In "Out of scope," change "Authorization is Shibi's, reserved." to "Authorization beyond authorship and the single-step delegation fact above is Shibi's, reserved; leases neither depend on Shibi nor constrain what it becomes." And keep "Transfer or delegation of a lease *between holders*" in the out-of-scope list unchanged — the new section's tenure/evidence distinction now gives that line its precise meaning.

The document then stands today: it prohibits the defect by behavior, defines the semantics of delegated evidence with a checkable rule, names the weakest sufficient mechanism without minting it, and identifies the exact line (delegation depth) at which Shibi becomes relevant — so Shibi's arrival, in either form, changes one noun in one sentence and rewrites nothing.

